  "articleSummary": "The article argues that agentic cyber threats emerge not from a single breakthrough but from multiplying two improving capabilities: agents’ autonomous task duration and orchestrators’ ability to coordinate many sub-agents. It cites METR’s task-horizon research, Anthropic’s reported GTG-1002 campaign using Claude Code, and Sysdig’s “LLMjacking” research to explain how attackers can exploit stolen cloud credentials to scale operations while victims pay the compute bills. Cheap, repeated attempts can turn low individual success rates into substantial aggregate risk, while defenders face costly human review. Aimed at security teams skeptical of agentic threats, the piece is a pointed, darkly humorous threat-model analysis framed through Terminator/Skynet metaphors, billing economics, and the contrast between a relentless individual robot and a coordinated swarm.",
